WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.130
Game of Thrones has an extensive and high-quality
rogue gallery.

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:08.400
I assume everyone probably has their own favorite
villain in the series since there are so many

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:13.770
great options, but for this video, I’m going
to be making the case for why Tywin is the

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:14.929
best villain in the show.

00:00:14.929 --> 00:00:19.780
I’m going to cover five main topics that
all combine together in making Tywin the best.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:24.120
And the first one is arguably the most important
in that, Tywin from his perspective in the

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.980
story is a good guy.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.930
Now I feel like this is a topic that comes
up all the time that entices actors towards

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:32.980
playing villains, and that’s within good
reason.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:36.610
Because villains that are essentially a good
guy, but just have different motivations than

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:40.010
our main protagonist, make more compelling
characters.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:44.340
This definitely doesn’t downplay the Joffreys
and Ramsays within Game of Thrones because

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.789
unpredictability and true evil intent can
also be terrifying.

00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:53.899
However, Tywin as an overall character is
more complex because he isn’t just causing

00:00:53.899 --> 00:00:55.270
chaos out of enjoyment.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:59.800
He’s making logical decisions that are deeply
tied to what motivates him.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:05.309
So looking at Tywin’s actions, he surprisingly
doesn’t do much bad things from his perspective.

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:10.439
Truly the only bad acts he did, was setting
the Riverlands on fire, which is understandable

00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:15.590
because it’s wartime, and the red wedding,
but his reasoning for doing so does make sense

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:18.189
and he saved lots of lives by ending the war.

00:01:18.189 --> 00:01:20.670
Albeit he obviously did it in a bad way.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:24.939
And he also hates Tyrion, but I wouldn’t
blame him from how much he disgraces the Lannister

00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:25.939
name.

00:01:25.939 --> 00:01:29.530
So because Tywin isn’t going out of his
way to cause harm and just wants a stable

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:35.100
realm that isn’t in constant rebellion,
it makes his character more real and relatable.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:39.770
We establish a deeper connection with Tywin
because we understand his goal and it’s

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.439
not compromised by constant heinousness.

00:01:42.439 --> 00:01:46.590
This allows the audience to build sympathy
and empathy for him and is essentially why

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:50.299
despite being a villain, there’s a lovable
aspect to him.

00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:54.399
Part of that reason is Charles Dances’ performance
which I’ll get into later, but the writing

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:57.009
for the character is pitch-perfect.

00:01:57.009 --> 00:02:01.579
A father trying his best to strengthen his
family name and is constantly rectifying all

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:05.180
the mistakes made by his children and grandchildren.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:09.030
Like the bad things he’s done wouldn’t
have been necessary had his family not have

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:11.069
made those issues so prevalent.

00:02:11.069 --> 00:02:15.459
I think a great set of scenes that helps with
sympathizing with Tywin and helps the audience

00:02:15.459 --> 00:02:19.270
look past some of these bad acts is his time
with Arya.

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:24.260
Them bonding together brings out an aura of
levity for Tywin which is also ironic because

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:27.870
he never gets this feeling when interacting
with his own children.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.660
And quite the opposite in fact.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:33.940
But with Arya, we peel back the curtain of
Tywin’s strict and hard persona of being

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:39.210
a commanding leader and we see a thoughtful
and caring man who surprisingly is so interested

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:40.310
in his cupbearer.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:44.570
I feel like during this showcase, if Tywin
was much more vile towards Arya maybe she

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:46.460
would have tried to assassinate him.

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:49.129
Even though the window was very small to do
so.

00:02:49.129 --> 00:02:53.430
I feel like it was partially ambiguous if
Arya hesitated since she kind of enjoyed her

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:54.880
time with Tywin.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.050
She was visibly upset when Tywin left Harrenhal
without her.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:02.460
David and Dan actually do a great job here
in humanizing Tywin and these scenes offer

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.799
a lot of value because nothing like these
scenes happen later in the show.

00:03:06.799 --> 00:03:11.209
From here on out he’s always going to be
a hard ass, primarily because going forward,

00:03:11.209 --> 00:03:13.959
he’ll be paired up with his children all
the time.

00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:17.940
So his scenes with Arya are that much more
special because we see a glimpse of the good

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:18.940
side of Tywin.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:22.549
They deepen his characteristics and thus make
him a more rich villain.

00:03:22.549 --> 00:03:27.599
That’s why to me, I have a slight preference
in this field for Tywin in comparison to the

00:03:27.599 --> 00:03:29.519
other villains of Game of Thrones.

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:35.590
He’s not just sympathetic, but he’s very
complex with justified and relatable motivations.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:40.280
Unlike any other villain, Tywin has a unique
set of traits that makes him very strong and

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.159
a worthy adversary.

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:45.610
Within in the show he by far has the most
influence and power.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:49.659
So this essentially gives him free range to
almost do anything he wants and that makes

00:03:49.659 --> 00:03:51.410
him scary to go up against.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:56.470
As a byproduct from this, this also makes
him very ruthless, intimidating, and he’s

00:03:56.470 --> 00:04:01.110
a very intelligent strategist given his mass
amount of experience and age.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.110
He is probably the best player in Game of
Thrones.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:08.620
He may not be as sleazy and maliciously backstabby
as Varys and Littlefinger, but their power

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:11.030
completely pales in comparison.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:15.670
Just seeing him walk into a room and immediately
seeing everyone fold over themselves to try

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:18.750
to curry favor with him is a sight to behold.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:24.270
He constantly exudes big dick energy and it’s
so much fun watching Tywin because of this

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:25.350
power.

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:28.510
Another reason why it’s so fun is the way
he overcomes conflict.

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:33.100
It’s always done through him dunking on
people verbally, completely outsmarting them,

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:37.720
and just holding so much authority over them
that they shatter into a million pieces.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:42.280
The only people that can sustain this crazy
beam of power that he unleashes when he speaks

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:46.250
are his kids, but that’s just because he
can’t outright banish them to the shadow

00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:47.250
realm.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:48.250
Anyone else though?

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:49.250
Good luck.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:53.060
Like him interacting with his random generals
in season 2 was hilarious.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:57.570
Any minor mistakes they made, they would immediately
get drop-kicked by Tywin.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:01.800
The times in which he is actually matched
in this cunning show of power also makes from

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.100
some incredible moments.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:07.370
I said it before in another video, but Olenna
and Tywin’s scene together where they talk

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:11.150
about marrying their kids is one of my favorite
fight scenes.

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:15.380
It perfectly highlights Tywin’s main strength
in that he’s a manipulative figure who’s

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:19.850
extremely intelligent and can bend political
mechanics to his will.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:23.690
Threatening to draft an order to make Loras
Tyrell a member of the Kingsguard if he doesn’t

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:28.160
marry Cersei is such a clever solution that
is practically uncounterable.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.680
Along with the ramifications of this deal
being massive.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:37.350
So just in one dialogue scene, Tywin completely
bests Olenna Tyrell and strengthens his overall

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.060
family position.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:43.060
This unique way of problem-solving is fresh
in Game of thrones because most characters

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:44.690
solve their problems through combat.

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:49.280
And to me, this type of solution to conflict
is so much more intriguing because it requires

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.290
wit, and thus keeps you engaged as a viewer
without needing action.

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:58.341
Along with it offering two other big factors
like variety in an already big show and the

00:05:58.341 --> 00:06:01.470
unpredictability of how Tywin will best someone.

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:05.550
Because when a protagonist fights an antagonist
in a sword fight, there are only so many outcomes

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:09.650
to that situation, and the way they carry
out that goal is always the same.

00:06:09.650 --> 00:06:14.100
But outsmarting your opponent via political
means creates a more interesting solution

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.460
that is more fresh and exciting.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:20.380
Another situation that I love where Tywin
was matched and even bested was by Tyrion

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.160
when he announces he wants a trial by combat.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:28.020
Throughout the show, Tywin gets his way and
his plans are always immaculate and go exactly

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.190
as he wants.

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:33.870
Jaime discovers that Tywin expected for him
to cave and to try and make a bargain to save

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:37.520
his brother in which he’ll take up the responsibilities
of his house.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:42.800
So as an audience, we assume that yet again,
Tywin has won in an intelligent way.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:46.880
But Tyrion has one final uno reverse card
and the staredown they give each other at

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:49.720
the end of the episode is truly iconic.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:54.490
It visually shows that they are true rivals,
and Tyrion, given how smart he is, can really

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:55.960
challenge his father.

00:06:55.960 --> 00:07:00.870
Plus his added immunity of being his son allows
for this intellectual battle to be more engaging

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:03.160
since Tywin can’t outright get rid of him.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:06.960
That’s why their scenes together are definitely
my favorite in the show and I’ll go into

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:08.840
more detail about them in a bit.

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:13.099
But briefly going into the ending of Tywin’s
character, it’s a plain and simple masterpiece

00:07:13.099 --> 00:07:14.099
from a writing standpoint.

00:07:14.099 --> 00:07:15.099
The most powerful character dying on a toilet.

00:07:15.099 --> 00:07:19.120
It’s a perfect ironic event given the character
at hand and the fact that it’s carried out

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.220
by Tyrion, explodes the scene with so much
emotion and release of tension.

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:28.250
So much buildup for Tyrion has led up to this
moment that it makes it a monumental death

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:30.600
scene despite it happening in the shitter.

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:34.330
I really can’t imagine a better place for
this type of villain to die.

00:07:34.330 --> 00:07:39.199
But yeah, as a villain Tywin’s characteristics
and the way he overcomes conflict with them

00:07:39.199 --> 00:07:45.630
makes him so engaging, and given those characteristics,
the way he dies is outstanding.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:50.190
Apart of how we quantify a villain’s worth
is how much they impact the plot and our main

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:51.490
characters.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:55.020
If you look at any great villain they’ll
generally check off both of these boxes in

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:56.020
a big way.

00:07:56.020 --> 00:08:00.550
A couple examples for the character aspect
include Ramsay torturing and thoroughly changing

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:05.879
the genetic make-up of Theon and Joffrey tormenting
Sansa while also making Tyrion go crazy with

00:08:05.879 --> 00:08:07.550
his terrible decisions.

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:10.800
Tywin has a major hold over Tyrion, Cersei,
and Jaime.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:15.879
So literally all of his scenes are so complex
and interesting because there is a rich dynamic

00:08:15.879 --> 00:08:18.630
playing out in all of them between him and
his children.

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:22.940
Like, try to think about it, almost all of
his most memorable scenes revolve around him

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:24.620
interacting with his children.

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:28.970
He’s deeply attached to them and they affect
him just as much as he affects them.

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:33.979
They are a constant stain on his legacy and
is more of a conflict to him than really anything

00:08:33.979 --> 00:08:34.979
else.

00:08:34.979 --> 00:08:39.089
Which is kind of funny given that he’s actively
fighting a war for three seasons.

00:08:39.089 --> 00:08:44.219
Now you may be thinking that Robb is Tywin’s
primary antagonist, but that is half true.

00:08:44.219 --> 00:08:50.070
On a plot basis, Robb is Tywin’s antagonist,
however, on a character basis, his children

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:51.070
are.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:56.209
Tywin values his family name above all else,
so his biggest obstacle is wrangling his children.

00:08:56.209 --> 00:09:00.920
Tyrion is a constant disappointment and an
embarrassment to the Lannister name, Cersei

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:05.589
is tired of being disrespected and no longer
wants to do as she’s told, and Jaime refuses

00:09:05.589 --> 00:09:08.820
to progress the family name with handsome
children.

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:12.060
Imagine wasting those godlike genes, holy
shit.

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:17.550
So because Tywin affects multiple main characters
drastically, it makes him extremely important

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:18.860
within the story.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:23.130
He sends them down massive character arcs
and is the main obstacle for all of them,

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:26.300
and in overcoming him, they evolve as characters.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:31.130
Like Cersei standing up to Tywin and threatening
to go public with the vile news of incest,

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:33.320
releasing her from his grip and giving her
freedom.

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:38.570
Tyrion killing his father and finally escaping
the emotional turmoil and manipulation ensnaring

00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:40.370
him his entire life.

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:44.810
And Jaime finally being able to live a life
of his choosing without his father breathing

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:46.120
down his neck.

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:50.300
So In terms of all of the villains, Tywin
has the largest reach in terms of character

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:51.430
impact.

00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:55.890
Because villains are generally more focused
and will specialize with challenging one character.

00:09:55.890 --> 00:10:00.820
But Tywin, he juggles three successfully while
also dealing with other characters briefly.

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:05.680
Apart of why some villains may come across
as dull like Euron Greyjoy, is that they don’t

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:07.410
challenge anyone on a character level.

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:12.040
There’s no deep insights the characters
have to evaluate about themselves in overcoming

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:13.040
him.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:15.140
So they never change as characters from said
conflict.

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:19.410
He’s just there to carry out certain plot
points and to supply a superficial obstacle

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.200
for the good guys to overcome.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:25.769
To be fair though, some villains physically
just can’t accomplish this since it wouldn’t

00:10:25.769 --> 00:10:29.810
make sense within the story to have the villain
and protagonist regularly have scenes together.

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:34.260
That’s why I think some villains like Cersei
became shallower in season 8 because they

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:36.730
didn’t have any protagonists to interact
with.

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:40.700
The first 4 seasons because of this was just
writing brilliance in which you had a perfect

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:44.160
mixture of protagonists and antagonists in
a single space.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:49.160
It was a constant hurricane of conflict and
Tywin was in the eye of the storm.

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:54.170
To me that was the embodiment of what made
Game of Thrones so amazing and Tywin’s effect

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:59.380
on everyone within Kingslanding increased
the depth significantly.

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:02.720
Going into the other half of the statement
where a villain has to heavily impact the

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:07.209
plot and main characters, let’s look at
how much Tywin affects the story.

00:11:07.209 --> 00:11:10.870
From an overall perspective, Tywin is a very
busy guy.

00:11:10.870 --> 00:11:15.240
He’s strategizing and leading a war, he’s
doing all of Joffrey’s work, he’s setting

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:19.660
up crucial alliances and pairing up marriages,
and he’s always working behind the scenes

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:22.360
like screwing over Robb Stark with the Red
Wedding.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:26.959
Even though he doesn’t go up against Robb
Stark, he’s actively challenging him off-screen.

00:11:26.959 --> 00:11:30.840
It definitely would have been cool to see
more of his battles with Robb Stark and him

00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:35.430
planning out different strategies, but unfortunately,
a lot of the battles during the war of the

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.920
five kings arc were cut for budget.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:42.500
Nonetheless, though, Tywin was still heavily
involved and this made him a very important

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:44.480
figure early on in the show.

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:48.490
The fact that through the coarse of his screen
time that he essentially opposes almost every

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:51.209
character is impressive from a writing standpoint.

00:11:51.209 --> 00:11:55.680
It’s like Ip Man fighting 10 black belts
at once but on a macro scale.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:59.519
Normally villains will just have a direct
tie to one subject at a time, which will thus

00:11:59.519 --> 00:12:03.000
increase the connection between them given
that you aren’t splitting time between a

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.100
bunch of things.

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:10.079
Tywin on the other hand, simultaneously affects
so much, but he never feels over-extended.

00:12:10.079 --> 00:12:14.650
The writers expertly utilize Tywin where it
matters most and a lot of that time is with

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:15.810
his kids.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:20.580
His macro problems don’t require many scenes
or could also be done efficiently like discussing

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:24.279
the war and then transitioning into a more
personal moment with his children like in

00:12:24.279 --> 00:12:25.329
season 1.

00:12:25.329 --> 00:12:29.170
It creates a great sense of efficiency with
the character in how they influence different

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:33.500
parts of the show and it never feels like
Tywin is being underutilized.

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:37.279
Like a really smart example of efficiency
is Tywin carrying out his work of hand of

00:12:37.279 --> 00:12:41.660
the king where he’s writing a letter, but
he’s doing so while Cersei is visiting.

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:46.000
So this creates a statement that Tywin cares
more about writing a letter than his own daughter.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:50.480
It’s extremely disrespectful and it knocks
out two birds with one stone.

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:54.579
Along with it returning later on in the show
where Cersei does the same tactic against

00:12:54.579 --> 00:12:55.800
Olenna Tyrell.

00:12:55.800 --> 00:13:00.360
The fact that Tywin affects so much of the
show, I think increases his value as a villain.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:05.190
It makes him more significant since anything
and everything he does carries so much weight

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:08.690
and causes ripples throughout the entire world
of Westeros.

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:13.380
The larger the impact they have the better
and Tywin created a massive tsunami with The

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:14.380
Red Wedding.

00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:18.500
Really the only other villain that comes close
in this regard is Joffrey killing Ned Stark,

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:22.120
and that completely shattered the realm and
the Red Wedding did the same.

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:26.420
However, I think Tywin consistently keeps
his pressure on the whole world more than

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:27.899
Joffrey does.

00:13:27.899 --> 00:13:32.940
Lastly and certainly not least, let’s finally
look at the performance of Charles Dance as

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:34.400
Tywin Lannister.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:37.480
This is the definition of an iconic and memorable
performance.

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:42.699
He requires lots of subtlety, gravitas, and
intimidation through sheer confidence.

00:13:42.699 --> 00:13:47.670
He isn’t a character who has a lot of emotional
outbursts, so you have to communicate a lot

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:49.180
with subtle acting.

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:53.610
The true gems of his performance are the minor
details that allude to certain things that

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:56.459
he doesn’t want to say or wants to suppress.

00:13:56.459 --> 00:14:00.590
The best example of this is his talks with
Tyrion where you can see the pure hatred in

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:01.590
his eyes.

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:06.110
There’s so much subtext and under-the-surface
mind games in their dialogue scenes, and this

00:14:06.110 --> 00:14:08.660
also alludes a lot to their backstory as well.

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:13.620
It’s so much fun picking up on all the small
details from these actors and it makes watching

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:14.880
them riveting.

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:19.279
Like Charles Dance can be extremely expressive
his eyes and eyebrows.

00:14:19.279 --> 00:14:22.970
He can go from one end of the spectrum where
he’s giving someone the death glare that

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:25.860
makes them melt, to something that can be
more lively.

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:30.529
I really love it whenever he squints at Tyrion
and you can just tell how pissed off he is.

00:14:30.529 --> 00:14:35.630
Charles Dance also paces character beats really
slow and allows them to build for dramatic

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:36.630
suspense.

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:41.120
He kind of turns it into a power play as the
opposing character awaits in fear about what

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:42.310
he’s going to say.

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:47.060
It allows his piercing glare to sink in longer
and get under their skin, thus breaking them

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:48.060
down.

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:51.259
A good example of this is whenever he puts
Joffrey in his place.

00:14:51.259 --> 00:14:55.230
He slowly tears into his soul with his death
glare and then rips out his heart when he

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:57.750
finally delivers his gut-wrenching line.

00:14:57.750 --> 00:15:02.930
This I think elevates the menacing factor
of Tywin and makes him an even stronger villain.

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:07.130
This slower-paced delivery also gives him
an heir of higher class, which upgrades his

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:08.320
badass degree.

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:13.480
He’s never groveling or breaking up to reduce
his strong overall composure, and very very

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:15.980
rarely does he ever show his emotions.

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:19.960
I think only once in the show does he actually
break down and that’s when he admits his

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:22.000
true disdain towards Tyrion.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:26.649
The moment he releases really floors you because,
in every scene leading up to this, he’s

00:15:26.649 --> 00:15:29.250
been very serious and closed off.

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:33.350
To me, it’s just very cathartic to see a
hardened character finally open up and Charles

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:35.959
Dance did an outstanding job in this scene.

00:15:35.959 --> 00:15:38.420
Out of all of them, it’s probably my favorite.

00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:42.410
Another classic aspect about the character
is obviously his commanding voice.

00:15:42.410 --> 00:15:47.560
It carries a deep, confident tone that demands
attention and respect.

00:15:47.560 --> 00:15:52.300
When he speaks, his voice is always measured
and deliberate, reflecting his calculated

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:53.620
and intelligent nature.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:57.910
It’s the literal embodiment of the character’s
power and he wields his authoritative deep

00:15:57.910 --> 00:16:00.029
voice as if it’s his sword.

00:16:00.029 --> 00:16:02.779
He cuts through every surrounding character
with it.

00:16:02.779 --> 00:16:06.899
You can’t help but love his voice and I
think it’s the primary reason why he’s

00:16:06.899 --> 00:16:08.389
a fan-favorite character.

00:16:08.389 --> 00:16:13.180
I can listen to his smooth voice for hours
and Charles Dance was really blessed by the

00:16:13.180 --> 00:16:14.620
genetic lottery.

00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:19.160
But still, though, the way he utilizes his
voice in the show excels Tywin Lannister into

00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:20.550
an iconic status.

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:24.910
His overall acting is amazing and perfectly
suits the character at hand.

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:28.880
Easily one of and if not the best casting
choice in the show.

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:33.480
So yeah, those five reasons are why he’s
my favorite villain in Game of Thrones, and

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:38.110
honestly, Joffrey I think is a very close
second since he also applies strongly to a

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:39.829
lot of these categories.

00:16:39.829 --> 00:16:42.470
Or even other villains like Cersei and Ramsay.

00:16:42.470 --> 00:16:47.399
The fact that we have this many great villains
is incredible and is a part of why Game of

00:16:47.399 --> 00:16:49.300
Thrones is one of the best shows.

